## Migration: `Thread.Abort` Timeout Pattern → .NET 8 Cooperative Cancellation

### Pattern Identified: **Timeout Enforcement**

Your code forcibly kills a thread via `Thread.Abort()` when it exceeds a deadline. In .NET 8, `Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` (SYSLIB0006). The replacement is cooperative cancellation with `CancellationTokenSource.CancelAfter`.

---

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class TimedExecutor
{
    public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
        var task = Task.Run(work, cts.Token);

        try
        {
            return task.WaitAsync(cts.Token).GetAwaiter().GetResult();
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException($"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
    }

    // Preferred: async overload
    public async Task<T> ExecuteWithTimeoutAsync<T>(Func<T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
        var task = Task.Run(work, cts.Token);

        try
        {
            return await task.WaitAsync(cts.Token);
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException($"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
    }

    // Best: when the work itself cooperates with cancellation
    public async Task<T> ExecuteWithTimeoutAsync<T>(
        Func<CancellationToken, T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
        var token = cts.Token;

        try
        {
            return await Task.Run(() => work(token), token);
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException($"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
    }
}
```

---

### Key Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Explanation |
|---------|-------------|
| **Cancellation isn't immediate** | Unlike `Thread.Abort`, cooperative cancellation only works if `work` checks the token. If `work` is a tight CPU loop that never yields, it won't stop until it finishes. |
| **Uncooperative work keeps running** | `Task.WaitAsync` lets your *caller* stop waiting, but the background task continues. If you must guarantee termination of uncooperative code, move it to a child **process** and use `Process.Kill`. |
| **Not disposing `CancellationTokenSource`** | Leaks timers. Always wrap in `using`. |
| **Catching `OperationCanceledException` too broadly** | Use `when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)` to distinguish your timeout from upstream cancellation. |
| **`ThreadAbortException` catch is gone** | The old `catch (ThreadAbortException) { Thread.ResetAbort(); }` has no equivalent — remove it entirely. |

---

### Making Work Cooperate (Critical)

For the cancellation to actually stop work, the delegate must check the token:

```csharp
var result = executor.ExecuteWithTimeoutAsync(token =>
{
    foreach (var item in items)
    {
        token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested(); // checkpoint
        Process(item);
    }
    return ComputeResult();
}, TimeSpan.FromSeconds(30));
```

Without these checkpoints, the task runs to completion regardless of the timeout — you only stop *waiting*, not the work itself.

---

### Summary of Removed APIs

| .NET Framework | .NET 8 Replacement |
|---|---|
| `thread.Abort()` | `cts.Cancel()` / `cts.CancelAfter(timeout)` |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | `catch (OperationCanceledException)` |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Check `token.IsCancellationRequested`, decide to continue |
| Dedicated `Thread` + `Join(timeout)` | `Task.Run` + `Task.WaitAsync(token)` |
